WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.860
Why can sprouting peas warm a thermos, while boiled peas cannot?

2
00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:06.280
Peas have no muscles.

3
00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:09.960
Both thermoses begin near room temperature.

4
00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:13.020
Only one contains living cells.

5
00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:18.700
Watch the warmth rise as we follow stored food energy through one pea cell.

6
00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:23.170
Inside the sprouting pea, food molecules store energy.

7
00:00:24.430 --> 00:00:26.170
The fuel we will follow is glucose.

8
00:00:27.650 --> 00:00:30.790
Glucose is a sugar that stores energy in its molecules.

9
00:00:32.290 --> 00:00:34.250
Plants make glucose by photosynthesis.

10
00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:38.450
Cells must release stored energy before using it.

11
00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:42.480
Cellular respiration releases that energy.

12
00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:45.940
Glucose and oxygen enter the pathway.

13
00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:51.020
Carbon dioxide and water leave, along with released energy.

14
00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:54.000
Oxygen is a gas cells use.

15
00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:57.620
Carbon dioxide is a gas they give off.

16
00:00:58.650 --> 00:01:02.370
Respiration happens in many small steps, not one burst.

17
00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:05.610
The first steps occur in the cytoplasm.

18
00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:09.630
Most of the work follows inside mitochondria.

19
00:01:11.030 --> 00:01:14.290
Plant and animal cells both have these small organelles.

20
00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:17.290
Fungus cells usually have them too.

21
00:01:18.350 --> 00:01:21.290
Do not confuse mitochondria with chloroplasts.

22
00:01:22.570 --> 00:01:25.730
Chloroplasts let a plant make glucose by photosynthesis.

23
00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:30.930
Mitochondria let plant and animal cells release stored food energy.

24
00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:34.670
A plant cell can contain both kinds of organelle.

25
00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:37.960
The pathway has released stored energy.

26
00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:40.740
Where will that energy go now?

27
00:01:41.980 --> 00:01:44.940
Will it power cell work, leave as warmth, or do both?

28
00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:47.240
Make your prediction.

29
00:01:53.233 --> 00:01:59.073
It does both. One branch powers cell work, such as building new cell parts.

30
00:02:00.353 --> 00:02:04.673
Another becomes thermal energy, the motion of particles we feel as warmth.

31
00:02:05.973 --> 00:02:09.413
Some released energy always leaves along this thermal branch.

32
00:02:10.417 --> 00:02:12.397
The cell did not make new energy.

33
00:02:13.657 --> 00:02:15.957
It changed energy already stored in glucose.

34
00:02:17.337 --> 00:02:20.597
The thermos slows the orange thermal energy from escaping.

35
00:02:21.977 --> 00:02:25.717
As living pea cells keep respiring, their thermometer can rise.

36
00:02:26.817 --> 00:02:29.717
Breathing is different from cellular respiration.

37
00:02:30.977 --> 00:02:32.957
Breathing moves air into and out of lungs.

38
00:02:34.317 --> 00:02:37.597
It brings oxygen in and carries carbon dioxide out.

39
00:02:38.957 --> 00:02:43.897
Respiration happens inside cells, including cells in organisms without lungs.

40
00:02:45.017 --> 00:02:47.017
Now compare the thermoses again.

41
00:02:48.497 --> 00:02:52.597
Sprouting peas contain living cells that keep releasing thermal energy.

42
00:02:54.017 --> 00:02:56.017
Boiling killed the cells in the control peas.

43
00:02:57.397 --> 00:03:01.917
After those peas cool, they do not respire, so their thermometer stays near the

44
00:03:01.917 --> 00:03:02.917
room.

45
00:03:03.367 --> 00:03:08.387
Respiration releases stored food energy for cell work and as thermal energy.

46
00:03:09.587 --> 00:03:12.487
That explains the warmth without inventing energy.

47
00:03:13.967 --> 00:03:18.867
Next, temperature, oxygen, and sugar will change its rate around the same pathway.
